<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Diagn Ther Endosc</journal-id><journal-title>Diagnostic and Therapeutic Endoscopy</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1070-3608</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1029-0516</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">18493545</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362823</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1070360800000266</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/DTE.7.35</article-id>	<article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Evaluation of Annuloaortic Ectasia by Angioscopy and IVUS &#x0201c;Report of 2 cases&#x0201d;</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>		<contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Tokuhiro</surname><given-names>Keiichi</given-names></name><address><email>tokuhiro@med.toho-u.ac.jp</email></address></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Uchida</surname><given-names>Yasumi</given-names></name></contrib>					<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kawamura</surname><given-names>Kouhei</given-names></name></contrib>					<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sakuragawa</surname><given-names>Hiroshi</given-names></name></contrib>					<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Masuhara</surname><given-names>Hiroshi</given-names></name></contrib>					<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Oosawa</surname><given-names>Hidefumi</given-names></name></contrib>					<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Koyama</surname><given-names>Nobuya</given-names></name></contrib>		</contrib-group>						<aff id="aff"><label/>				<addr-line>Cardiovascular Center</addr-line>				<addr-line>Toho University Hospital at Sakura</addr-line>				<addr-line>564-1 Shimoshizu</addr-line>				<addr-line>Sakura city, Chiba</addr-line>				<addr-line>285-8741</addr-line>				<country>Japan</country>			</aff>			<pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>7</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>35</fpage><lpage>45</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>15</day><month>5</month><year>2000</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>4</day><month>7</month><year>2000</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>4</day><month>7</month><year>2000</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2000 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>			<p>We attempted combined use of angioscopy and intravascularultrasonography (IVUS) to localize the coronary ostia anddetermine the aortic segment to be replaced in patients withannuloaortic ectasia, because these preoperative informations areimportant for selection of an appropriate technique forreconstructing the coronary artery, to prevent complications, andalso to postoperative follow-up. Two cases with annuloaorticectasia underwent angioscopy and IVUS both pre- andpost-operatively. Structure of aortic cusps, position of coronaryostia, the extent of ectasia with very thin wall were clearlyobserved by IVUS. Angioscopy showed milky white luminal surface ofthe ectasic segment. After Cabrol&#x02019;s operation, the sutured portionof native aorta and graft was clearly identified by IVUS and muralthrombus and naked surface of graft were observed by angioscopy.Complications were observed in none. The results indicatefeasibility of combined use of angioscopy and IVUS fordetermination of surgical approach and follow-up in patients withAAE.</p>		</abstract></article-meta></front></article>